1
00:00:01,133 --> 00:00:03,600
When you order bubble tea and upgrade from medium to large

2
00:00:03,633 --> 00:00:05,833
the page says it's only three yuan more

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,033
You casually add boba and a cheese foam topping

4
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:09,800
just six yuan extra in total

5
00:00:10,033 --> 00:00:11,633
That first sip does feel satisfying

6
00:00:11,766 --> 00:00:16,133
but by the end the ice melts the drink turns cloyingly sweet and half the cup is left

7
00:00:16,233 --> 00:00:17,766
Six yuan isn't much

8
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:22,133
yet as you toss the cup you still wonder whether those add-ons were worth it

9
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:24,633
Most people don't overspend because they can't do the math

10
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:30,000
The problem is we mistake the total still looks fine for adding a bit more is also a bargain

11
00:00:30,166 --> 00:00:32,233
Marginal analysis is about exactly this

12
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:35,966
It doesn't ask how much you've bought how long you've worked or how many times you've invested

13
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:37,566
It only watches the next one

14
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:41,433
This sip of tea this extra half hour at work this ten-yuan cart filler

15
00:00:41,433 --> 00:00:45,400
Does the extra benefit they bring still match the extra price you pay

16
00:00:45,966 --> 00:00:49,733
The order page's favorite line is just a little more

17
00:00:49,966 --> 00:00:51,400
Large is only three yuan more

18
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:52,866
Toppings only two yuan more

19
00:00:52,966 --> 00:00:55,133
Add ten yuan more and delivery is free

20
00:00:55,133 --> 00:00:57,966
These tiny numbers rarely trigger any alarm

21
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:01,433
because they sit next to a total you've already accepted

22
00:01:01,733 --> 00:01:04,200
You were already ready to buy the 38-yuan tea

23
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:07,333
so adding three more doesn't feel like a new decision

24
00:01:07,433 --> 00:01:10,233
It feels like just finishing off the original one

25
00:01:10,433 --> 00:01:11,733
But think about it

26
00:01:11,733 --> 00:01:13,000
The medium cup is one decision

27
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:14,833
Upgrading to large is another

28
00:01:14,966 --> 00:01:16,933
Adding boba is yet another

29
00:01:17,133 --> 00:01:19,766
Each step trades new money for new things

30
00:01:20,133 --> 00:01:23,066
They shouldn't hide behind well I'm buying anyway

31
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:28,233
That vague guilt when you check your bill at month's end often starts right here

32
00:01:28,333 --> 00:01:30,466
Not from one terrifyingly big purchase

33
00:01:30,533 --> 00:01:32,400
but from every purchase being just a little more

34
00:01:32,633 --> 00:01:34,933
Swapping a single meal for a combo

35
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:38,066
Tossing a small item into the cart to hit a discount

36
00:01:38,433 --> 00:01:40,833
Upgrading a video membership from monthly to yearly

37
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,533
Agreeing to one more revision right before you get off work

38
00:01:43,733 --> 00:01:46,866
Each one alone has a perfectly reasonable-sounding excuse

39
00:01:47,233 --> 00:01:53,266
Put together you'll notice money time and patience leaking away through a pinhole

40
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:58,200
The tricky part is we use what we've already spent to justify what comes next

41
00:01:58,433 --> 00:02:01,266
The tea is ordered anyway so might as well go large

42
00:02:01,366 --> 00:02:05,666
The cart is already at eighty so might as well add twenty for free shipping

43
00:02:05,833 --> 00:02:10,833
The deck is already done by nine so might as well stay up one more hour to polish it

44
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:16,633
But the tea you ordered the eighty you spent the pages you wrote are all in the past

45
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,733
They can't automatically prove the next step is worth it too

46
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,800
What you're hesitating over was never whether the whole cup is worth forty yuan

47
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:29,233
You're hesitating over whether the last three yuan can buy that exact bit of satisfaction you want

48
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,366
The difference looks tiny

49
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:35,533
but it redraws the boundary on choices that run on autopilot

50
00:02:35,966 --> 00:02:38,066
Some things are very useful the first time

51
00:02:38,233 --> 00:02:39,633
still decent the second

52
00:02:39,933 --> 00:02:43,200
by the third you may just be avoiding stopping

53
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:46,866
With some work one round of edits makes the plan much clearer

54
00:02:46,966 --> 00:02:52,833
By round four nudging a button two pixels left may just burn a night meant for sleep

55
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,200
You don't need to blame yourself for this

56
00:02:55,533 --> 00:02:59,033
Our brains naturally treat a continuous stretch as one single thing

57
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:02,266
But life's bills are rarely settled in one lump sum

58
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:04,466
They add up one small step at a time

59
00:03:04,633 --> 00:03:06,800
What to watch out for isn't wanting more

60
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:11,266
It's failing to check whether that extra bit still gives you anything new

61
00:03:11,766 --> 00:03:14,833
Marginal sounds like a word from a textbook

62
00:03:15,033 --> 00:03:17,000
It really just means the increment

63
00:03:17,333 --> 00:03:20,666
You already have some What happens when you add one more

64
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:24,066
In economics marginal analysis splits this into two sides

65
00:03:24,233 --> 00:03:26,033
One side is marginal benefit

66
00:03:26,166 --> 00:03:30,633
the extra gain from doing this one more time or buying one more unit

67
00:03:30,966 --> 00:03:32,800
The other side is marginal cost

68
00:03:32,833 --> 00:03:39,566
what you additionally pay for that new unit money time energy even how you feel the next day

69
00:03:39,833 --> 00:03:43,733
When deciding you shouldn't compare total benefit against total cost

70
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:47,333
You should compare the next unit's marginal benefit against its marginal cost

71
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:51,566
If those three extra yuan take you from not enough to just right

72
00:03:51,766 --> 00:03:53,933
its marginal benefit may be high

73
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:59,366
If you already can't finish a large and it just leaves more melted ice at the bottom

74
00:03:59,533 --> 00:04:01,933
then those three yuan don't buy much

75
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:05,066
This doesn't mean everything loses value as you add more

76
00:04:05,233 --> 00:04:08,733
Sometimes the second one is worth more than the first

77
00:04:09,166 --> 00:04:12,566
Moving house alone the first friend who shows up matters a lot

78
00:04:12,733 --> 00:04:16,733
The second friend brings a van and suddenly the hardest stretch is solved

79
00:04:16,966 --> 00:04:20,233
Now one more person actually adds more benefit

80
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:23,533
So marginal analysis isn't just stop when it's enough

81
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:26,800
Nor is it telling you to deny yourself every pleasure

82
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,566
It only asks that when you add more you don't let the first one vouch for the last one

83
00:04:32,766 --> 00:04:35,233
This applies especially to overtime

84
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:38,533
At seven pm you spend another half hour finishing the materials

85
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:42,533
If it makes tomorrow's meeting run smoothly that half hour may be worth a lot

86
00:04:42,966 --> 00:04:46,866
At eleven pm you decide to recolor every single slide

87
00:04:47,133 --> 00:04:48,933
The slides may look a bit neater

88
00:04:48,933 --> 00:04:54,300
But you're trading away sleep tomorrow's brain and inviting careless mistakes

89
00:04:54,300 --> 00:04:57,566
In the first half hour marginal benefit beats marginal cost

90
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:00,533
In the later hour the ledger may have already flipped

91
00:05:00,633 --> 00:05:01,600
Same overtime

92
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:02,866
Same half hour

93
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:05,100
They are not the same deal

94
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:07,400
This is where marginal analysis shines

95
00:05:07,433 --> 00:05:09,733
It doesn't let one big total hold you hostage

96
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:14,666
You don't have to say I've put in three hours so the fourth must follow

97
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:19,433
Nor I've already spent money today so one more item is fine

98
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:21,866
Every addition can be judged anew

99
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:23,833
Like walking up to a buffet

100
00:05:24,033 --> 00:05:25,733
The first plate feeds you

101
00:05:25,833 --> 00:05:27,233
the second is for pleasure

102
00:05:27,366 --> 00:05:34,333
If the third only brings the misery of overeating it isn't justified by how good the first two tasted

103
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:37,266
Much of life's bad value isn't from starting too late

104
00:05:37,333 --> 00:05:41,000
but from pushing past the stopping point with the reasons we began with

105
00:05:41,233 --> 00:05:44,566
Friday night a familiar urge hits many people

106
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:47,033
It's been a long week just one more episode

107
00:05:47,366 --> 00:05:49,766
The first episode may cost only forty minutes

108
00:05:49,933 --> 00:05:53,600
It unwinds you from work and gives your brain a new channel

109
00:05:53,766 --> 00:05:56,600
With the second bedtime is getting close

110
00:05:56,833 --> 00:05:59,533
After the third tomorrow's wake-up gets harder

111
00:05:59,966 --> 00:06:00,933
Same episode length

112
00:06:00,933 --> 00:06:03,200
but what they crowd out is completely different

113
00:06:03,566 --> 00:06:06,200
The later episode's marginal cost isn't just forty minutes

114
00:06:06,366 --> 00:06:12,000
It also takes your morning energy and a slow breakfast you could have had on the weekend

115
00:06:12,233 --> 00:06:14,033
Most people don't lack self-control

116
00:06:14,233 --> 00:06:18,033
They're still judging by the first episode's cost when they hit play on the next

117
00:06:18,166 --> 00:06:19,266
Shopping works the same way

118
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:23,133
You meant to buy one box of laundry pods cart at seventy-two yuan

119
00:06:23,333 --> 00:06:26,566
The page says add eight yuan more and the six-yuan delivery fee is waived

120
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:28,733
This is when a certain phrase appears

121
00:06:28,900 --> 00:06:30,200
It's only eight yuan more

122
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:33,266
But you're not comparing seventy-two with eighty

123
00:06:33,400 --> 00:06:34,833
That's a comparison of totals

124
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:41,600
What you should compare is that eight-yuan item versus the six-yuan fee which works out better for you

125
00:06:41,933 --> 00:06:46,233
If it's something you'll actually use next week buying it is fine

126
00:06:46,533 --> 00:06:52,800
If it will sit in a drawer for a year you're paying two yuan extra to bring home something that takes up space

127
00:06:53,433 --> 00:06:55,333
Free shipping isn't always cheaper

128
00:06:55,566 --> 00:07:00,066
It just disguises a standalone decision as being one step from a discount

129
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:02,566
Studying falls into this trap too

130
00:07:02,766 --> 00:07:04,766
You sit down at night to write a proposal

131
00:07:04,900 --> 00:07:08,466
The first twenty minutes of research clarify the problem

132
00:07:08,633 --> 00:07:11,833
Another half hour finds you a few better examples

133
00:07:12,033 --> 00:07:16,133
But by hour three you're on the seventh article saying the same thing

134
00:07:16,166 --> 00:07:18,200
At that point you're not adding understanding

135
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:20,566
You're probably delaying the first sentence

136
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:22,566
More material later start

137
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:24,400
Not because you aren't serious

138
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:28,066
but because new material no longer brings as many new insights

139
00:07:28,166 --> 00:07:30,033
Its marginal benefit has shrunk

140
00:07:30,133 --> 00:07:33,400
And its marginal cost has become the night you never started writing

141
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:35,700
AI tools amplify this feeling

142
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:37,633
First you ask it for ten directions

143
00:07:37,733 --> 00:07:41,233
Picking two out of ten is usually helpful

144
00:07:41,233 --> 00:07:46,233
But then you have it rewrite twenty headlines outline fifty plans list a hundred examples

145
00:07:46,233 --> 00:07:47,866
Options seem to multiply

146
00:07:47,933 --> 00:07:50,466
while the actual step you must take keeps sliding further back

147
00:07:50,766 --> 00:07:51,933
The tool isn't the problem

148
00:07:52,233 --> 00:07:57,433
The problem is when new answers only make choosing harder they no longer save you effort

149
00:07:57,566 --> 00:08:00,466
Marginal analysis doesn't say shut it all down right now

150
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:02,733
It offers a lighter reminder

151
00:08:03,100 --> 00:08:10,366
Next time before searching more editing more adding more ask does this new batch bring me closer to done

152
00:08:10,700 --> 00:08:14,333
If not it may just be another detour that looks like hard work

153
00:08:14,533 --> 00:08:17,833
That's why the last stretch tends to cost the most

154
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:19,600
Its price tag hasn't changed

155
00:08:19,733 --> 00:08:24,200
But the time attention and energy you have left already have

156
00:08:24,433 --> 00:08:29,400
The scarcer your resources the more the same small addition costs you

157
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:34,800
Marginal analysis at its most practical isn't about carrying a calculator through life

158
00:08:35,166 --> 00:08:38,400
It just asks you to pause ten seconds before adding a little more

159
00:08:38,833 --> 00:08:41,266
Don't first ask how much I've already spent

160
00:08:41,533 --> 00:08:43,800
Ask what the next one gives me

161
00:08:44,033 --> 00:08:46,000
About to go large Then ask

162
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:48,833
Will I really finish the extra part of this cup

163
00:08:49,100 --> 00:08:50,966
About to pad the cart Then ask

164
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,366
Was this new item already on my shopping list

165
00:08:54,733 --> 00:08:57,266
About to work another half hour Then ask

166
00:08:57,366 --> 00:09:02,066
Will it solve a real problem or just make stopping harder

167
00:09:02,533 --> 00:09:07,400
About to read one more article or ask AI for one more version Then ask

168
00:09:07,533 --> 00:09:11,333
Does it move me toward delivery or just delay facing the start

169
00:09:11,533 --> 00:09:13,466
These questions have no standard answers

170
00:09:13,566 --> 00:09:15,966
Sometimes adding more is worth it

171
00:09:16,300 --> 00:09:18,633
You're genuinely thirsty the large gets finished

172
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,233
The cart filler is needed next week

173
00:09:21,433 --> 00:09:24,666
The half hour of overtime heads off tomorrow's big problem

174
00:09:24,833 --> 00:09:28,766
In these cases the added benefit still beats the added cost

175
00:09:29,100 --> 00:09:29,900
Then add it

176
00:09:30,300 --> 00:09:33,833
But if your only answer is well I've come this far

177
00:09:33,833 --> 00:09:35,766
that phrase should make you pause

178
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:38,266
Because it isn't about whether the next one is worth it

179
00:09:38,366 --> 00:09:40,833
It says the previous one is already spent

180
00:09:41,133 --> 00:09:42,333
The past matters of course

181
00:09:42,333 --> 00:09:43,966
But it can't be changed

182
00:09:44,033 --> 00:09:50,000
What you still hold is the next three yuan the next half hour and a bit of unused attention

183
00:09:50,300 --> 00:09:53,566
Saving them for places that still have real gains isn't being a killjoy

184
00:09:53,833 --> 00:09:57,033
It's keeping your limited resources from being eaten away by inertia

185
00:09:57,133 --> 00:10:00,600
Next time you see only three yuan more on the order page

186
00:10:00,733 --> 00:10:02,600
try translating that line

187
00:10:02,900 --> 00:10:04,133
Not just three yuan more

188
00:10:04,166 --> 00:10:08,033
But do I want to trade three yuan for those last few sips

189
00:10:08,533 --> 00:10:10,600
If the answer is yes enjoy the drink

190
00:10:10,766 --> 00:10:13,666
If you hesitate going back to medium is no big deal

191
00:10:13,800 --> 00:10:15,400
You haven't lost out on anything

192
00:10:15,433 --> 00:10:18,866
You just refused to let the first sip's pleasure decide for the last sip

193
00:10:18,966 --> 00:10:21,366
Next time we'll talk about the equimarginal principle

194
00:10:21,733 --> 00:10:28,633
When money and time both run short how do you split them across choices without feeling shortchanged either way
